<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conference of Ambassadors</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Conference of Ambassadors was a body of the Allied powers set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1920 to enforce and interpret the peace treaties after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Made up of the ambassadors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under a French chair, it settled frontier questions: in November 1921 it confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>'s 1913 borders, ending Yugoslav backing for the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirdita">Mirdita</a>, and in 1923 it fixed the Polish–Lithuanian border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors.jpg" alt="Conference of Ambassadors" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roke, Hoshie · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council-of-Ambassadors-map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Conference of Ambassadors of the Principal Allied and Associated Powers was an inter-allied organisation of the Entente, formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in January 1920 as a successor to the Supreme War Council. Its task was to enforce the peace treaties that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and to mediate territorial disputes between European states. It was later incorporated de facto into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> as one of its governing bodies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Conference was made up of the ambassadors of the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> accredited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, together with the French minister of foreign affairs, who chaired it. Among its chairmen were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a>, Raymond Poincaré and Aristide Briand. The ambassador of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> attended only as an observer, since the United States was not an official party to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. The French diplomats Jules Laroche and René Massigli served as its secretaries-general, and a series of committees and commissions, some permanent and some ad hoc, advised them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The disputes brought before the Conference included Cieszyn Silesia, contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, the Vilnius Region between Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, the Klaipėda Region between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Lithuania, and the Corfu Incident between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. On 15 March 1923 it recognised the eastern borders of Poland that had emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-soviet-war">Polish–Soviet War</a> of 1920, together with Polish sovereignty over the Vilnius region and Eastern Galicia.</p><p>At a meeting in March 1925 Marshal Foch briefed the Conference on German disarmament. He reported that the old Imperial German Staff still existed, that the German army stood at 500,000 men rather than the 100,000 allowed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, that surplus war materials had been found, that no law limited the size of the armed forces, and that the police force of 150,000 men was kept on a military footing. Foch asked for a treaty of security between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom, but the British ambassador, Lord Crewe, blocked further discussion. The Conference became less active after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno-treaties">Locarno Treaties</a> of 1925.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conference of Ambassadors” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The League of Nations tried to mediate, and Paul Hymans proposed plans for a Polish–Lithuanian union, but the <b>Conference of Ambassadors</b> awarded Vilnius to Poland in March 1923 and Lithuania broke off all relations; the two countries remained officially at war over the city until Lithuania accepted the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Conference of Ambassadors</b> in London made Albania an independent principality under the Great Powers' guarantee and chose the German Prince Wilhelm of Wied as ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-albania">Principality of Albania</a></li><li><blockquote>Albanian government forces, with commanders including Ahmet Zogu, crushed the revolt in November 1921, while the <b>Conference of Ambassadors</b> confirmed Albania's 1913 borders, ending Yugoslav claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mirdita">Republic of Mirdita</a></li></ul></section>
